    "result_text": "The only test class is `AcceptanceTest`, and it passes. The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved date-derived values to a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July, named by the calendar year in which it ends, e.g. a Sept 2026 date \u2192 FY27).\n\n**`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a reusable domain helper `Owner.fiscalYear(LocalDate)` that returns the fiscal year for a date (`month >= July \u2192 year+1`, else `year`), returning `null` for a null date. Added the `java.time.Month` import.\n\n**`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`**\n- Added a new read-only `fiscalYear` mapping + `formatFiscalYear` method returning `FY<YY>` derived from the (already business-day-adjusted, persisted) registration date.\n- Changed `formatMembershipNumber` so the `-M<YY>` year segment uses the fiscal year instead of the registration date's calendar year.\n- Changed `formatMembershipPoints` tenure rule from \"more than 365 days\" to \"one or more elapsed fiscal years\" (`fiscalYear(now) - fiscalYear(registrationDate) >= 1`).\n- Removed the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import.\n\n**`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the `fiscalYear` read-only string property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the accessor the mapper targets), and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis.\n\nVerification: `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The audit log confirms the new behaviour for today's date: `membershipNumber=NSW-16E6609E-M27` (registration 2026-09-30 \u2192 FY27).\n\nOne design decision worth flagging: the spec gives the format `FY<YY>` but not whether the fiscal year is named by its starting or ending calendar year. I used the Australian/ATO convention (named by the ending year \u2014 1 Jul 2026\u201330 Jun 2027 = FY27), applied consistently across `fiscalYear`, `membershipNumber`, and the tenure calculation, and documented it in the Javadoc and OpenAPI descriptions. The only test asserts the `FY\\d{2}` format, so this choice is internal-consistency driven \u2014 let me know if you'd prefer the start-year convention (which would make today's value FY26).",
