    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and follows the patterns already established in this codebase:\n\n- **`Owner.fiscalYear(LocalDate)` placement is natural.** It sits directly alongside the existing static, pure date helpers `toBusinessDay` and `isWeekend` in `Owner`. Making it a static `Integer`-returning method that null-guards its argument mirrors `toBusinessDay` exactly, so a maintainer would expect date-derivation utilities to live right here. The computation (the fiscal-year rule) correctly lives in the domain model, not the mapper.\n\n- **`formatFiscalYear` follows the mapper convention.** It's a `@Named(\"formatX\")` default method with a null guard and a Javadoc matching every sibling formatter (`formatMembershipNumber`, `formatCheckDigit`, etc.), and it's wired via the same `@Mapping(..., qualifiedByName=...)` idiom. The mapper stays a formatting/presentation layer that delegates the actual year derivation to `Owner`.\n\n- **No inappropriate duplication.** `formatFiscalYear` and `formatMembershipNumber` share the small `Owner.fiscalYear(...) % 100` expression, but each wraps it in its own distinct format string (`\"FY%02d\"` vs `\"%s-M%02d\"`), and this \"each formatter stands alone\" style is exactly how the surrounding methods are written. Extracting a shared helper would fight the file's established grain rather than help it.\n\n- **`formatMembershipPoints` change is a clean in-place substitution** of the old `ChronoUnit.DAYS` comparison for the fiscal-year delta, with the stale `ChronoUnit` import removed and the Javadoc updated to match. No new responsibility accreted onto the method.\n\n- **The OpenAPI additions are additive and consistent** with the neighboring read-only computed fields.\n\nLogic is in the right units, nothing is becoming a catch-all, and the additions read like the existing code.",
